2026-06-30 15:24:39,448 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-bd3dfc53-57a0-406c-934a-a9f5d02d6aad', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n220203_Alex_BioID__D_D_16_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__D_D_14_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__D_D_18_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__D_D_22_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__D_P_14_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__D_P_16_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__D_P_18_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__D_P_22_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__M_D_14_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__M_D_16_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__M_D_18_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__M_D_22_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__M_P_14_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__M_P_16_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__M_P_18_AmBic.raw\n220210_AB_BioID__M_P_22_AmBic.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nProteasomeID: quantitative mapping of proteasome interactomes and substrates for in vitro and in vivo studies\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nProximity labeling coupled to mass spectrometry enables in situ mapping of protein-protein interactions. Here, we have developed a strategy based on tagging of proteasomes with promiscuous biotin ligases and a newly generated mouse model to monitor the interactome of proteasomes in vivo. We demonstrate that biotin ligases can be incorporated in fully assembled proteasomes without negative impact on proteasome activity. Analysis of proteins labeled by tagged proteasomes retrieved more than half of the known proteasome-interacting proteins in a single mass spectrometry analysis, including assembly factors, activators and ubiquitin-cycle related proteins. We optimized the protocol for processing of proximity labeled samples to minimize contamination from streptavidin and implemented Data Independent Acquisition (DIA) mass spectrometry for label-free analysis. We demonstrate the utility of our workflow by identifying novel proteasome-interacting proteins, charting interactomes across mouse organs, and showing that proximity-labeling can be used to identify both endogenous and small molecule-induced proteasome substrates.\n\n[METHODS]\nCell pellets were resuspended in 4.75 ml of lysis buffer (50 mM Tris pH 7.5; 150 mM NaCl; 1 mM EDTA; 1mM EGTA; 1% (v/v) Triton X-100; 1 mg/ml aprotinin; 0.5 mg/ml leupeptin; 250 U turbonuclease; 0.1% (w/v) SDS), followed by 1 h incubation in the rotator mixer (15 rpm) at 4°C to aid the lysis. Samples were then briefly sonicated in a Bioruptor Plus for 5 cycles (30 s ON/30 s OFF) at high setting and afterwards centrifuged at 20817 x g, for 30 min at 4°C to remove any debris. Cleared lysates were transferred to new tubes, 50 µl of streptavidin coated beads added, and samples were incubated for 3 h on the rotator (15 rpm) at 4°C. This was followed by centrifugation at 2000 x g for 5 min at 4°C and removal of 4.5 ml of the supernatant from each sample. Remaining sample with the beads at the bottom was transferred to a Pierce Spin Column Snap Cap column and the tubes were additionally rinsed with lysis buffer and added to the Pierce Spin Column Snap Cap column. Beads were then washed on the column with a lysis buffer, followed by 3 washes with freshly prepared 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (AmBic), pH 8.3. The bottom of the columns were closed with a plug and beads transferred to fresh 2 ml tubes by means of 3 x 300 µl 50 mM AmBic, pH 8.3. The samples were then centrifuged at 2000 x g for 5 min at 4°C and the content of each tube was removed until around 200 µl of the liquid (including the bead) remained at the bottom. 1 µg of LysC was added and incubated at 37°C for 16 h shaking at 500 x rpm. The samples were then centrifuged at 2000 x g for 5 min at room temperature and the content of the tubes were transferred to Pierce Spin Column Snap Cap columns. The digested peptides were eluted with two times 150 μl of freshly made 50 mM AmBic. To elute biotinylated peptides still bound to the beads, 150 μl of 80% ACN and 20% TFA was added, briefly mixed, and rapidly eluted. This elution step was repeated twice and the eluates merged. Following elution, 0.5 μg of trypsin was added to the AmBic elutions and digestion continued for an additional 3 h with mixing at 500 x rpm and 37°C. Digested AmBic elutions were then dried down in a vacuum concentrator, resuspended in 200 µl 0.05% (v/v) formic acid in milliQ water and sonicated in a Bioruptor Plus (5 cycles with 1 min ON and 30 s OFF with high intensity at 20°C). ACN/TFA elutions were dried down in a vacuum concentrator (not to completeness but until approximately 50 µl were left), 50 µl of 200 mM HEPES pH 8.0 were added to the samples and pH adjusted to 7-9. 0.5 μg of trypsin were then added and digestion continued for an additional 3 h with mixing at 500 x rpm and 37°C. Digested peptides were acidified with 10% (v/v) trifluoroacetic to pH <3. Both AmBic and ACN/TFA elutions were desalted using Macro Spin Column C18 columns following manufacturer’s instructions and dried down in a vacuum concentrator.Prior to analysis, samples were reconstituted in in MS Buffer (5% acetonitrile, 95% Milli-Q water, with 0.1% formic acid) and spiked with iRT peptides (Biognosys, Switzerland). Peptides were separated in trap/elute mode using the nanoAcquity MClass Ultra-High Performance Liquid Chromatography system (UPLC) or nanoAcquity UPLC system (Waters, Waters Corporation, Milford, MA,USA ) equipped with a trapping (nanoAcquity Symmetry C18, 5 μm, 180 μm × 20 mm) and an analytical column (nanoAcquity BEH C18, 1.7 μm, 75 μm × 250 mm). Solvent A was water and 0.1% formic acid, and solvent B was acetonitrile and 0.1% formic acid. 1 µl of the sample (∼1 μg on column) were loaded with a constant flow of solvent A at 5 μl/min onto the trapping column. Trapping time was 6 min. Peptides were eluted via the analytical column with a constant flow of 0.3 μl/min. During the elution, the percentage of solvent B increased in a nonlinear fashion from 0–40% in 90 min. Total run time was 115 min including equilibration and conditioning. The LC was coupled to an an Orbitrap Exploris 480 (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Germany) using the Proxeon nanospray source. The peptides were introduced into the mass spectrometer via a Pico-Tip Emitter 360-μm outer diameter × 20-μm inner diameter, 10-μm tip (New Objective) heated at 300 °C, and a spray voltage of 2.2 kV was applied. For data acquisition and processing of the raw data Tune 3.1 and Xcalibur 4.4  were employed.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
